17592186045417] [17592186157275 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186157079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157275 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5d4ea352-2a82-5ff5-8c08-b7f9a09929e6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157480 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157480 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157480 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186158131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157480 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157480 #edb/ref 1045 "The Absorption Laws" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157480 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186158131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157480 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157480 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186157480 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "8bdf85c1-4b83-5092-87f5-18958edf4721" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158101 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158102 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158101 #edb/ref 1061 26063 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158101 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "06b73134-c888-557b-b24b-5c077c2e7029" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158101 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158102 #edb/ref 1061 26064 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158102 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "bfff8667-4509-537f-8b85-edfe60cfa421" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158102 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186157275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158131 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186158101 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158131 #edb/ref 1061 26413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158131 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "46368002-98d2-5228-8269-a1aea4e09ac3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158131 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186158482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158482 #edb/ref 1064 "There are two laws, called the **absorption laws**, that can be used to drastically simplify some logical expressions.\n\n- The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nA \\land (A \\lor B) \\equiv A.\n$$\n\n- Likewise, the law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nA \\lor (A \\land B) \\equiv A.\n$$\n\nSometimes, the laws are stated more concisely as\n\n$$\nA \\land (A \\lor B) \\equiv A \\lor (A \\land B) \\equiv A.\n$$\n\nTo prove that these laws hold, we can construct truth tables:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,B\\,$ | $\\, A \\land (A \\lor B) \\,$ | $\\, A \\lor (A \\land B) \\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm T$ |\n| $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |\n| $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ | $\\color{blue} \\textrm F$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158482 #edb/ref 1065 4344 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158482 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "dcc7f471-b2da-52b6-b003-76ae7ca1e64a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186158482 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378181 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186471830 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378181 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186471831 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378181 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186471832 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378181 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103060" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378181 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378181 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $E?$\n- **I.** $F∧(F∨E)$\n- **II.** $E∧(F∨E)$\n- **III.** $E∨(F∨E)$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378181 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3546d6ca-c236-59ab-90e2-5cf833951b0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378181 #edb/ref 1074 "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nE∧(E∨F)≡E.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is *not* logically equivalent to $E.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F∧(F∨E) & ≡F≢E.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $E.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}E∧(F∨E) & ≡E∧(E∨F) \\\\  & ≡E.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is *not* logically equivalent to $E.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378202 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186471839 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378202 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186471840 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378202 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186471841 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378202 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103068" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378202 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378202 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $C?$\n- **I.** $¬D∧(C∧¬D)$\n- **II.** $C∧(¬D∨C)$\n- **III.** $(¬D∨C)∧C$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378202 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f7d8581e-cdcd-55c5-92e2-f687cc9d64c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378202 #edb/ref 1074 "The law that conjunction absorbs disjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nC∧(C∨¬D)≡C.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is *not* logically equivalent to $C.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $C.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C∧(¬D∨C) & ≡C∧(C∨¬D) \\\\  & ≡C.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is logically equivalent to $C.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬D∨C)∧C & ≡C∧(¬D∨C) \\\\  & ≡C∧(C∨¬D) \\\\  & ≡C.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378223 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186471848 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378223 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186471849 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378223 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186471850 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378223 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103320" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378223 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378223 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $L?$\n- **I.** $L∨(¬M∧L)$\n- **II.** $(L∧¬M)∨L$\n- **III.** $(¬M∧L)∨L$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186378223 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d3034ba1-2c82-5776-b118-ddbefff303ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186378223 #edb/ref 1074 "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nL∨(L∧¬M)≡L.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $L.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L∨(¬M∧L) & ≡L∨(L∧¬M) \\\\  & ≡L.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $L.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(L∧¬M)∨L & ≡L∨(L∧¬M) \\\\  & ≡L.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is logically equivalent to $L.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(¬M∧L)∨L & ≡L∨(¬M∧L) \\\\  & ≡L∨(L∧¬M) \\\\  & ≡L.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380166 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472343 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380166 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472344 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380166 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472345 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380166 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81721" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380166 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380166 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $P?$\n- **I.** $P∨(Q∧P)$\n- **II.** $(Q∧P)∨P$\n- **III.** $P∨(Q∨P)$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380166 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "34117327-fd37-5989-b657-502be019389b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380166 #edb/ref 1074 "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nP∨(P∧Q)≡P.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is logically equivalent to $P.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P∨(Q∧P) & ≡P∨(P∧Q) \\\\  & ≡P.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $P.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Q∧P)∨P & ≡P∨(Q∧P) \\\\  & ≡P∨(P∧Q) \\\\  & ≡P.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is *not* logically equivalent to $P.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380173 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472346 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380173 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472347 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380173 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186472348 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380173 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380173 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380173 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following are logically equivalent to $M?$\n- **I.** $M∨(N∨M)$\n- **II.** $(M∧N)∨M$\n- **III.** $M∨(N∧M)$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186380173 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c524db33-b009-501a-aa7b-2de183456b8d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186380173 #edb/ref 1074 "The law that disjunction absorbs conjunction is stated as\n\n$$\nM∨(M∧N)≡M.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is *not* logically equivalent to $M.$ This statement cannot be simplified using the absorption laws.\n\n- Statement II is logically equivalent to $M.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(M∧N)∨M & ≡M∨(M∧N) \\\\  & ≡M.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is logically equivalent to $M.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M∨(N∧M) & ≡M∨(M∧N) \\\\  & ≡M.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407253 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407273 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186407253 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186407